WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.360
Malcolm Grant: Paper on prioritising patients
with every decision we take.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:09.610
I should and I apologise, at the outset attended
the apologies of Tim Kelsey whose paper it

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.769
is but who is not able to be with us today
but Bill McCarthy is going to introduce the

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:14.769
paper.

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:20.830
Bill McCarthy: Thank you chairman, this report
steps out some of the practical steps we’re

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:26.080
taking to make our commitment around involving
patients in every decision we take and at

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.689
the heart of all we do, putting that into
practice.

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:37.190
The paper covers a whole range of activities,
some of which we have discussed before at

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.559
the Board, so I do not intend to take you
through paragraph by paragraph.

00:00:41.559 --> 00:00:48.400
However, I would pick out two important areas.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.649
The first is around patient and public involvement
in specialised commissioning; this is covered

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:55.660
in paragraphs 16 and 17.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:03.680
You will recall, Chairman, that in our readiness
for the financial year just started, we had

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:10.550
to undertake a pretty rapid process of preparing
for specialised commissioning.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:19.250
I think we were understandably criticised
for the degree to which we could really involve

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:28.110
patients and the public during that pretty
short period, and I think the comments that

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.030
were made to us were justified and fair.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:36.390
We think it is really important that we draw
from that and start working now to put into

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:42.220
place much fuller and more comprehensive arrangements
for the following year.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:48.920
Those commitments now are set out in paragraph
17, to respond to what I think were some fair

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.420
points that were made to us.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:58.590
The other area I want to draw out is right
at the start of the paper; it is in paragraphs

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:06.800
two and three, around widening digital participation
for all.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.360
At this stage, this is a relatively contained
project; but I think it is quite an important

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:12.360
one.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:19.450
We have talked at the Board about our view
that digital channels of communication are

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:26.250
going to be increasingly important, both for
empowering patients, involving patients, as

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:31.790
well as for giving out the right sort of information
and support to patients.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:38.420
Therefore, it is important for us that we
assure that is a channel of communication

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:45.120
that can be available to as many people and
different parts of our communities as possible,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.580
and we know that there are some parts of our
communities who have poorer access to digital

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.460
communications than others.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:03:00.430
This project is about tackling that and working
with the Foundation in partnership to provide

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:06.270
areas, provide access to groups who could
be disadvantaged to digital communication.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:12.510
I just wanted to highlight that, because it
goes with a number of our commitments about

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.700
equality and fairness for all.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:24.170
The only other thing I would say is that Victor
or Ciarán may want to talk briefly about

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:29.319
the piece of work they are leading around
the Civil Society Assembly that we mentioned

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:31.300
at last Board.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.730
With those three points, Chairman, I would
ask the Board to note the report.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:36.270
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Bill.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:40.020
Victor and Ciarán, you had an invitation.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:48.670
Victor Adebowale: Ciarán and I have started
on this idea of building what has been in

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:53.170
very, very draft terms called the Civil Citizen
and Community Engagement.

00:03:53.170 --> 00:04:03.670
I have to say that I have taken the view that
we need to progress this in a mode of respectful

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:04.670
enquiry.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:10.600
The reason for that is that there are lots
of organisations out there that would perceive

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.550
that they do some of this.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:22.169
We have started to have discussions with organisations
like Healthwatch, for instance, who were launched

00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:31.259
yesterday; National Voices; and many other
not-for-profit groups.

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:34.610
We are in the process of establishing a steering
group.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:42.229
We have a staff support setup, but I think
the design of this is really, really important,

00:04:42.229 --> 00:04:48.990
because if we get it right, then we start
to describe what a health and social care

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.669
movement looks like and what it can do.

00:04:51.669 --> 00:04:58.340
That relates to the point that David made
about the constitution.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:02.610
If we get it wrong, then we look a bit like
the René Magritte painting when the guy looks

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.159
in the mirror and sees the back of his head.

00:05:05.159 --> 00:05:12.979
We need to be really clear that the way we
do this is honest and transparent, and that

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:16.870
we are able to react to what we see.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:23.229
That is a big promise we are making, so I
think our process has to be really carefully

00:05:23.229 --> 00:05:27.270
managed and led, for which I am very grateful
for Ciarán’s support in taking on and putting

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:28.270
his name to this as well.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:29.969
Malcolm Grant: Ciarán, do you want to add
to that?

00:05:29.969 --> 00:05:39.620
Ciarán Devane: No, not hugely, but can I
talk about one or two of the others?

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:43.949
I think it is great that there is a response
to criticism and engagement with specialised

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:49.190
services, and I think it is back to the principle
that if we are going to do something like

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:53.830
this, responding to what we hear is important,
so I commend that.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.849
Another small comment around Friends and Family
Test.

00:05:56.849 --> 00:06:01.210
What does not quite come across from the paper
is that there is a lot of other work going

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:04.619
on as well; but how you respond to the Friends
and Family Test?

00:06:04.619 --> 00:06:07.840
That just tells you that you have a problem,
potentially; it does not tell you what to

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:08.840
do about it.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:15.499
Tim is working with a number people, including
us, on that aspect as well, around saying,

00:06:15.499 --> 00:06:21.280
‘If I do pick up something, what was good-quality
experience, and what is the intervention that

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.280
will improve it?’

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:23.280
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:24.280
I have Ed and Margaret.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:25.280
Ed Smith: Just a point on paragraph seven
and the Insight Dashboard.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.620
I think it would be helpful in the light of
the earlier discussion to ensure that the

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:42.139
executives have their own assurance, and can
assure the Board, not just about the full

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:48.430
functionality and public availability of it,
but the resilience of that system.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:07:00.629
I would much rather us be very comfortable
with its resilience than run ahead of ourselves

00:07:00.629 --> 00:07:03.699
on that.

00:07:03.699 --> 00:07:07.270
Malcolm Grant: It is worth noting, thank you.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:08.270
Margaret.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:09.270
Margaret Casely-Hayford: ‘Prioritising Patients
In Every Decision That We Make’ is such

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:10.270
a great title and such a great objective,
and I think that as the Friends and Family

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:13.449
Test rolls out, it is going to be a really
good way of understanding whether in fact

00:07:13.449 --> 00:07:16.479
we are achieving our objective.

00:07:16.479 --> 00:07:25.689
I do feel though that the Friends and Family
Test needs to be underscored – I have said

00:07:25.689 --> 00:07:33.900
this before and I will keep on saying it – by
our understanding of how staff feel in the

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:37.120
implementation of everything that they need
to do.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:50.210
We cannot always focus on the end-game without
understanding how we are going to get there.

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:58.909
If staff are not happy, not feeling listened
to, not feeling fulfilled, no matter how hard

00:07:58.909 --> 00:08:07.919
we ask ourselves whether the Friends and Family
Test is a good test, we would achieve the

00:08:07.919 --> 00:08:13.469
objectives much more readily if we are asking
ourselves not to have the Friends and Family

00:08:13.469 --> 00:08:15.259
Test as a single totem.

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:22.389
I just feel that you can move from a target
totem to a Friends and Family Test totem and

00:08:22.389 --> 00:08:26.559
forget everything else that is attached to
it.

00:08:26.559 --> 00:08:31.509
We can see that if you have the targets as
a totem, care and compassion could be something

00:08:31.509 --> 00:08:32.509
[break in audio].

00:08:32.509 --> 00:08:52.000
I think what I am saying is, let us not be
too totemic about the Friends and Family Test.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.320
Malcolm Grant: Yes.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.110
Victor, I think you wanted to come in, and
David?

00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:17.579
Victor Adebowale: I just want to 

00:09:17.579 --> 00:09:23.130
add to that, really.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:33.810
I do not think it is an either/or; it is an
and/and.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:39.630
I just wanted to add that the and/and bit
is two little clues as to what is possible.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:49.620
One is the Six Cs, which I think, from what
I have observed, have really released some

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:52.980
energy out there for a positive shift.

00:09:52.980 --> 00:10:00.670
The other is the NHS Change Day, which I thought
was incredibly powerful in creating a sense

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:02.209
of possible.

00:10:02.209 --> 00:10:04.269
I think that is really important.

00:10:04.269 --> 00:10:09.410
If you add that to what Margaret has just
said, you start to create conditions in which

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:18.209
we can really start to work with staff and
patients, consumers, as opposed to for, or

00:10:18.209 --> 00:10:19.850
even, indeed, against.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.089
I think there is something really positive
out there.

00:10:22.089 --> 00:10:30.320
The NHS Change Day and the Six Cs are clues
to what this could look like.

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:31.720
Really good stuff, I thought.

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:33.240
Malcolm Grant: Good, thank you.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:34.240
David?

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:37.600
David Nicholson: I absolutely agree with you,
Margaret, and in a sense, we will be coming

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:40.829
onto this when we talk about the business
plan.

00:10:40.829 --> 00:10:48.670
If you look in the business plan, the headline
is ‘Happy Patients, Happy Staff.’

00:10:48.670 --> 00:11:09.940
They do go together, and it is even 

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:44.670
more important, and you identified that 
it 

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:54.610
was [break in audio] junior doctors talking
together saying, ‘Junior doctors complain

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:59.939
and moan about things, would it not be good
if every junior doctor did something positive

00:11:59.939 --> 00:12:01.911
about improving quality on a particular day?’

00:12:01.911 --> 00:12:09.779
That grew and grew and grew, so that on the
day, 182,000 people signed up.

00:12:09.779 --> 00:12:13.740
There was no target; it grew bottom up.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:20.170
In fact, 11,500 people in Northern Ireland
signed up who were not even part of it.

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:24.310
It just shows you there that if you just get
the thing right, if you get the right support,

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:29.589
how you can release a huge amount of talent,
energy and enthusiasm to improve services

00:12:29.589 --> 00:12:30.589
for patients.

00:12:30.589 --> 00:12:32.750
A really good example.

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:33.750
Malcolm Grant: Good, well thank you.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.730
On that note, I propose that we note the report
– Thank you very much, Bill

